                                    "text": "================================================================================\ntom_loopback_membrane — Parameter Information\n================================================================================\n\nWHAT IT IS\nA triggered tom. Four drum-head modes (circular-membrane Bessel ratios) are\neach a loopback-FM oscillator: at 0 % fm it is a real-sounding modal tom,\nand turned up every mode grows its own harmonic series, a \"space\" tom\nhalfway to a synth. The pitch glides down because the head loses energy\n(tension follows energy, as in a real tom). A roll pushes it back up. A\nfiltered noise burst gives the stick or felt attack. The upper modes are\npanned out, so the drum fills the stereo field without moving its\nfundamental off centre.\n\nA click on in2 is a hand laid on the head: it chokes the ring quickly,\nfrom a gentle fingertip fade to a hard palm grab with a dull thump. The\nthump only happens while the tom is sounding.\n\nEvery hit lands on a slightly different spot (mode balance, detune, pan\ntilt, attack colour drift by a random walk). Mode phases run freely, so\nno two hits line up the same. A retrigger ramps each mode from where it\nis: no reset, no click.\n\nSource: Hsu & Smyth, SMC-19 §3.4, §4.1, §5.3, §5.5, §6.2 (loopback-FM\nmodal percussion, tom tom); Marogna, Avanzini & Bank, DAFx-10 P49 §2.2,\n§3.2 eq. 20, §4 (membrane modes, energy-based tension modulation, hammer);\nAvanzini & Rocchesso, DAFx-01 §2.1 (contact time).\n\nPARAMETERS (8 + output gain)\n\n1. tune_hz — 35…800 Hz, default 110 Hz\n   Head fundamental. 35-60 = sub boom; 60-90 = floor tom / dub boom;\n   150+ = rack tom; 400+ = timbale / syndrum ping. The level is compensated\n   below ~40 Hz (K-weighted loudness within 1.6 dB from 35 to 800 Hz), and\n   the fm harmonics thin out at high tunings so they never alias.\n\n2. decay_ms — 100…2500 ms, default 600 ms\n   Time to -40 dB for the fundamental; upper modes die sooner.\n\n3. glide_st — 0…12 st, default 3 st\n   Pitch rise at full energy; the fall follows the decay. 0 = steady;\n   12 = 808-style \"pew\" tom.\n\n4. fm — 0…100 %, default 35 %\n   Loopback-FM brightness. It is strongest at the attack and fades into the\n   tail. 0 = acoustic modes; 100 = buzzy, synthetic.\n\n5. mallet — 0…100 %, default 40 %\n   Felt (0: 4 ms contact, round and dark) to stick (100: 0.4 ms, more\n   upper modes and a louder noise attack).\n\n6. spread — 0…100 %, default 40 %\n   Stereo spread of the upper modes. 0 = mono.\n\n7. variance — 0…100 %, default 35 %\n   How much the strike spot wanders from hit to hit. 0 = same spot every\n   time (the free-running phases still differ).\n\n8. hand — 0…100 %, default 40 %\n   The muting hand on in2. Fingertips (0) ease the drum out in about\n   200 ms with no sound of their own. A firm palm (100) chokes it in about\n   12 ms with an audible dull thump. The thump follows how loud the tom\n   still is: up to about -11 dB re the stick hit right after a hit, quieter\n   as the ring dies, silent on a silent drum. The upper modes die first, as\n   under a real hand. The hand stays down until the next stick hit lifts\n   it.\n\n9. out_gain_db — -24…+12 dB, default 0 dB\n   Final trim, last in the chain.\n\nINLETS / OUTLETS\n- in1: trigger. A single-sample click (any rising edge above 0.1). No\n  velocity: the level is fixed, and variance supplies the movement.\n- in2: hand mute. A single-sample click (rising edge above 0.1) lays the\n  hand on the head; see \"hand\". Muting a silent drum makes no sound.\n- out1 / out2: stereo.\n\nNOTES\n- Isolated hits peak around -3…-1 dBFS at defaults; fm and mallet at 100\n  run about 4 dB hotter.\n- No safety limiter.\n",
                                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                                }
                            }
                        ],
