                                    },
                                    "text": "param out_gain_db 0 @min -24 @max 12",
                                    "varname": "out_gain_db"
                                }
                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "id": "obj-38",
                                    "linecount": 61,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 577.0, 1197.0 ],
                                    "text": "================================================================================\nkick_808_tension — Parameter Information\n================================================================================\n\nWHAT IT IS\nA triggered kick. A sine body starts sharp and falls to its pitch as its\nenergy drains, like a tensioned head (or an 808's bridged-T). At the hit the\nwaveform carries a loopback-FM harmonic series, so the kick still reads on\nphone speakers. The series thins to a clean sine as the note dies, which\nkeeps the low end tight instead of boomy. A soft contact pulse gives a felt\nbeater thump plus a mid \"knock\", lowpassed so it never clicks.\n\nEvery hit lands on a slightly different spot (detune, brightness, knock\npitch, contact time drift by a random walk). Hitting a drum that is still\nringing ramps from where it is: the pitch starts a little higher (energy\nadds) and the contact is shorter and softer. No reset, no click.\n\nSource: Werner, Abel & Smith, DAFx-14 (TR-808 bass drum: §4 pulse shaper,\n§8.1 attack frequency shift, §8.2 pitch sigh); Marogna, Avanzini & Bank,\nDAFx-10 P49 §2.3, §3.2 eq. 20 (tension ~ energy); Hsu & Smyth, SMC-19 §3.2,\n§3.4 eq. 8-9 (closed-form loopback FM); Avanzini & Rocchesso, DAFx-01 §2.1\n(contact force).\n\nPARAMETERS (7 + output gain)\n\n1. tune_hz — 35…800 Hz, default 50 Hz\n   Pitch the body settles to. 45-55 = deep dubstep; 60-70 = trip-hop thud;\n   150+ = tom-like, then synth-blip territory. The level is compensated\n   across the range (K-weighted loudness within 3.6 dB from 35 to 800 Hz),\n   and the harmonics thin out at high tunings so they never alias.\n\n2. decay_ms — 80…1500 ms, default 380 ms\n   Time to -40 dB. Under 250 = tight and punchy; over 800 = 808-style boom.\n\n3. drop_st — 0…36 st, default 14 st\n   How far above tune_hz the hit starts. 0 = flat sine; 24+ = laser zap.\n\n4. drop_ms — 5…200 ms, default 35 ms\n   How fast the pitch falls. Short = punch; long = sighing, dubby kick.\n\n5. harmonics — 0…100 %, default 45 %\n   Loopback-FM brightness at the attack, plus gentle saturation. This is\n   what phones and laptops hear. 0 = pure sine.\n\n6. beater — 0…100 %, default 40 %\n   Beater level and hardness: soft felt thump to hard plastic knock.\n\n7. variance — 0…100 %, default 35 %\n   How much the strike spot wanders from hit to hit. 0 = identical hits.\n\n8. out_gain_db — -24…+12 dB, default 0 dB\n   Final trim, last in the chain.\n\nINLETS / OUTLETS\n- in1: trigger. A single-sample click (any rising edge above 0.1). No\n  velocity: the level is fixed, and variance supplies the movement.\n- out1 / out2: the kick (mono, same on both).\n\nNOTES\n- Isolated hits peak near -2 dBFS at defaults; fast rolls stack higher.\n- No safety limiter.\n",
                                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                                }
                            }
                        ],
